# Ansible managed
# The systemd --user copy of notify-failure@.service (B2-CONTRACT.md §5, role mc_notify_client, scope user), for user
# units such as the gaming PC's timers. A user unit opts in with
#   OnFailure=notify-failure@%n.service
# and each failure of it runs this user's own `mc-notify --unit=<that unit>` (the "=" keeps a name starting with "-"
# from reading as an option) with this user's own config and token (both 0600 in ~/.config/mc-notify). Never from a
# catch-all drop-in (service.d/): notify-failure@ would get an OnFailure= too and loop. The system copy's sandbox is left out on purpose: most of it needs privileges a user manager
# doesn't have, and a setting the manager can't apply fails the unit, which would silence the notice. No
# network-online.target here either: the user manager has no such unit.
[Unit]
Description=Push "%i failed" to the phone through Mission Control (user manager)
Documentation=file:///home/noah/.local/bin/mc-notify

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment="MC_NOTIFY_CONFIG=/home/noah/.config/mc-notify/config.json"
ExecStart=/home/noah/.local/bin/mc-notify --unit=%i
TimeoutStartSec=60
SyslogIdentifier=mc-notify
UMask=0077
NoNewPrivileges=yes
